    "turn_text": "Hello, and welcome to Madison's Notes, the official podcast of Princeton University's James Madison program in American ideals and institutions. I'm your host, Annika Nordquist. Today, I'm incredibly excited to introduce a member of Princeton's class of 1992, although you probably know him better as the Senator from Texas, Ted Cruz. Senator Cruz really needs no introduction. In addition to representing the 30 million residents of the state of Texas, he has authored dozens of legislative measures that have been signed into law. He graduated from Harvard Law School and became a clerk to Chief Justice William Runquist. At Princeton, he studied under our director, Professor Robert P. George. And as a member of the American Wind Clioscopic Society, he became one of the top debaters in the country. Today, we're here to talk about his new book, Unwoke How to Defeat Cultural Marks as an In America, which is linked in the show notes. This is a really timely book, in part for us here at the Madison program, because we're doing a series on free speech. So in this conversation, we're going to talk about the book, we're going to focus especially on free speech, and we're going to talk about Senator Cruz's time here at Princeton. So with no further ado, I hope you enjoy. Senator Cruz, welcome to the show. It's such an honor to have you.",
