    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "So I have lots of wonderful memories from Princeton. I had an incredible time. I was class of 92. I actually tell a lot of the stories in the book about being at Princeton. When I went to Princeton, it was a world I didn't know. No one in my family had ever gone to an Ivy League school. I didn't come from money. My dad was an immigrant from Cuba. My mom was from a working-class family. She was the first in her family ever to go to college. And there are lots of stories I tell. But what one I would say is my favorite that I do tell in the book is I was very active in debate. I was very active in Wake Claya. And my best friend was a guy named David Panton. He was my roommate at Princeton. And we were debate partners. And David is Jamaican. And he's from Jamaica. He loves Jamaica. And I describe a debate tournament. We usually debated with each other. But there was one tournament where he and I split up. And it was what was called a hybrid tournament. So you paired up with someone from a different school. And I was debating with a guy named slash who went to University of Maryland Baltimore County and slash. Had long hair and red beard and more leather jacket that was festooned with razor blades. He had thirty seven razor blades. He said one for every every woman that had had broke his heart. And he was a man of the left. And he and I were debating together. In fact, we debated as Armageddon A because it was a fairly unlikely linking of slashing me together. But we're in the round and I described how one of the things that David, my roommate, he and I would often do in debates is we got tired of people whining. that when you ran a debate",
